 a certain content  could be content they get money from  could be content they maybe even own  watching certain content doesn't count  towards your monthly data cap and that  is yet another way that an ISP can favor  some cons  and over other content and if the bill  ultimately passes Internet service  providers will not be able to get  government contracts in California  without sticking to the rules that are  passed and this is a really important  thing to understand states can't enforce  net neutrality directly as of right now  relates to commerce law there is a  lawsuit over that but as of right now  states can't just generally enforce net  neutrality law what states can do is to  set rules for what ISPs that they work  with have to do and this relates to  something called the market participant  doctrine we don't have to get into the  details of that but the way that states  can sort of effectively enforce net  neutrality is by setting requirements  that ISPs have to meet in order to get  contracts with the state what this sort  of means de-facto is that since an ISP  is not going to have one set of rules  for their state customers and another  set of rules for all other customers in  the state the state and individual  consumers basically just become  consumers and you've got the same rules  for everybody but what does it possibly  mean if the state laws start being  adopted in multiple states well that's  the idea right if you see multiple  states adopt laws that require some  version of net neutrality in order to  get state contracts you might see ISPs  just say you know what let's just make  this policy federally it is an uphill  battle it's a major one but it's one  that I think is worth fighting because  how important this issue is right I've  talked to you about the meta issue of  net neutrality and I'm going to keep you  posted about other states that are  working on similar bills right now  legislators in 29 states have introduced  more than 58 bills 